3-3 Insn Fetch and Branch Control

Insns fetched from I-cache are first latched in a buffer called Insn
Word Queue (IWQ).  IWQ consists of 6 words x 2 'sides'.  Those sides are 
called A side and B side, switching from one to the other on conditional
branches.  Each side has its own Next Insn Pointer (NIP) and Insn Fetch
Address (IF-AD) registers.  Effective ID (EID) tells which side is 
currently being used.  In D stage, 2 words that are in the current side
and addressed by the corresponding NIP are taken out of IWQ and fed to
the exec pipeline.

[diagram here]

Cache data is always fetched into the rightmost two words of IWQ.  Every
time data is fetched, IWQ is shifted left by two words.  This action is 
called Shift-up.

Insn fetch is always done in 8 bytes and on 8-byte boundary.  Therefore
a branch to addresses like xx4 or xxC will inhibit a superscalar execution
right after the branch, because only one effective insn word is received
from I cache in the first target fetch.  If possible, compilers should
align branch target addresses to 8-byte boundary.

In STM arch, branch insns are classified into 3 categories: A) PC-relative
unconditional, B) PC-relative conditional, C) GR-relative unconditional.
Furthermore, A) and C) have a linking option.  We call PC-relative branches
"branches", and GR-relative ones "jumps" (which may not be a widely 
accepted convention).  We don't have conditional branches with link or
conditional jumps.

Control flow insns are handled in three ways:

A) conditional branches with cc not yet decided ('undecided branch')

	Issue a target fetch in D stage using the opposite side of
	the current ID.  Branch prediction is done statically; if the
	offset of a branch insn is positive, it is predicted not to be
	taken, and insn fetching continues with the current side.
	Otherwise it is predicted to be taken, EID is switched to the
	other side, and insn fetching starts on the new EID from the
	next cycle.

B) unconditional branches, or conditional branches with cc already
   available ('decided branch')

	ID is not changed.  NIP of the current side is cleared and
	a target fetch is issued using that side in D stage.  The
	fetch in IB stage at this cycle is cancelled, because it is
	for those insns which follow the branch and therefore will
	not be executed.

C) jumps
	
	Cancel IB stage and clear NIP in D stage.  The target address
	is not yet available in D stage.  Issue a request in A stage
	using the current ID.

SU interface of the IF pipe is different from that of the exec pipe.
SU returns CLH, but not RDY.  When IWQ is full (i.e. NIP<=1), IU does
not receive the fetched insn words even if CLH is being sent, because
there's no place to retain the words.

It may look weird at first sight, but  it does work if IU issues insn
fetch requests and addresses as follows:

o if a jump is in A stage,
	IU issues a request with the target address.

o elif a branch is in D stage,
	IU issues a request with the target address.

o elif a jump is in D stage,
	IU does not issue a request.

o elif IB stage is valid and IB-ID == EID
	IU does not issue a request.

